// §14 entry 12 -- a capability-bearing implementation constructed behind a
// bare `dyn` -> EXS-E0510.
//
// §4.4: "`dyn Trait poscit P`. Constructing a trait object from an
// implementation whose mark exceeds `P` is EXS-E0510." A bare `dyn Trait`
// (no `poscit` at all) is bound to the EMPTY row, so ANY capability-bearing
// implementation exceeds it. §4.3's own example: "A type with a
// capability-typed field, transitively, is capability-bearing... structura
// ScriptorRetis { sock: rete } // capability-bearing: [rete]" -- reused
// directly below; §4.3 also settles (commit 4edb704) that this mark is
// *computed* from the fields, never separately declared, so there is
// nothing else to keep in sync with the `sock: rete` field.
//
// REWRITTEN against checker.md finding 20 and the §8.6 decisions the same
// commit ratified, which is what the ORIGINAL fixture got wrong three ways
// at once -- and CLAUDE.md is explicit that a case which fails for the
// wrong reason "hides the bug it claims to catch":
//
//   1. Arity/receiver (§8.6 decision 8, finding 6). There is no `self` and
//      no `Self`: a member of an `interfacies` (or of an `interfacies X in
//      T` block) is an ordinary `Signature` whose FIRST parameter is the
//      receiver -- typed as the interface's own name (`Scriptor`) in the
//      interface, meaning "the implementing type," and as the concrete
//      type (`ScriptorRetis`) in the implementation. `x.m(a)` is `m(x, a)`.
//      The original `scribe(t: textus)` had arity 1 with no receiver at
//      all. Below, `scribe(s: Scriptor, t: textus)` in the interface and
//      `scribe(s: ScriptorRetis, t: textus)` in the impl match that rule.
//   2. No impl. The original fixture declared `Scriptor` and
//      `ScriptorRetis` but never associated the two, so there was no
//      implementation for the checker to find a mark on at all. Below,
//      `interfacies Scriptor in ScriptorRetis { … }` (§8.6 decision 4:
//      implementation reuses `interfacies`, one token -- `in` -- after the
//      head decides) supplies it.
//   3. No cast. `redde s;` returned a bare `ScriptorRetis` where `dyn
//      Scriptor` was declared, with no conversion at all -- caught as an
//      ordinary type mismatch (a class-030x code) before ever reaching
//      `EXS-E0510`, since Exsecutor has no implicit conversions (§4.5,
//      §5.2, §5.4, §6.3). §8.6 decision 4 makes the trait-object cast
//      explicit and postfix: `w sicut dyn Scriptura poscit {rete}` is the
//      form; below, `s sicut dyn Scriptor` is that cast with the row left
//      off, i.e. bound to the empty row -- which is the escape this entry
//      exists to catch. This is now the ONLY error the fixture contains:
//      the receiver arity matches, the impl exists, and the value is
//      explicitly, not implicitly, converted to `dyn Scriptor` -- so the
//      one thing left for the checker to object to is that `ScriptorRetis`'s
//      computed mark, `{rete}`, exceeds the bare cast's bound, `{}`.
//
// `interfacies Scriptor` is not a name collision with any prelude
// `Scriptor` (`examples/imprime.exsc` uses one without declaring it): §12
// pre-seeds prelude names in a scope outside the module's own, and a
// module's own declaration of the same name shadows it there, so this
// fixture's local `Scriptor` is exactly what a real module would write.
//
// `-> mensura`, not `-> eventus<mensura>`: §11 now says `Scriptor.scribe`
// should report failure as `eventus<mensura>`, since a closed descriptor is
// only discovered at the write and a bare `mensura` cannot report that --
// but marks both `examples/imprime.exsc` and this entry `[OPEN]` until
// `eventus` is inhabited. The spelling is not the obstacle (an earlier
// version of this paragraph said it was): the checker interns `eventus<T>`
// today; what it lacks is any variant, constructor, pattern or layout
// (docs/design/sum-types.md section 2), and the prelude row still returns
// the count. Kept as `-> mensura` here for exactly that reason, not
// overlooked.
//
// STALE, corrected here: the paragraph this replaces said the capability
// checker did not exist and that compiler/x86_64/ast/ and cst/ were both
// still empty. Neither has been true for a while, and CLAUDE.md's evidence
// discipline is explicit that a header misstating its own blocker is a false
// claim, not a rounding error. Measured with a fresh `exsc` from the working
// tree, `--diagnostica json`, against THIS file (the receiver-arity fix
// above already applied): `{EXS-E0302, EXS-E0305, EXS-E0510}`. `EXS-E0510`
// fires exactly at `s sicut dyn Scriptor`, span-correct (§4.4) -- the
// capability-bearing-type tracker and `dyn` bound checker this entry tests
// both exist and both work.
//
// FIXED here: `EXS-E0302` was `ast/from_cst.inc`'s `__ast_w_iface` (the
// implementation branch) giving `AST_D_IMPL` the TRAIT's own name
// (`Scriptor`) instead of no name, contradicting the invariant
// `resolve.inc`'s `__chk_mods` states immediately above the check it
// tripped ("an `Impl` head introduces no name") -- `interfacies Scriptor in
// ScriptorRetis { ... }` collided with `Scriptor`'s own declaration. Fixed
// in `from_cst.inc`; pinned by `tests/unit/ast_from_cst_impl_noname.asm`.
// Same defect, same fix, as §14 entry 24's fixture.
//
// STILL BLOCKED, and NOT by the capability checker: `EXS-E0305`
// ("operation not defined on the type"), TWICE over, both in
// `checker/types/` (Stage 2), neither touched by this fix:
//
//   - `t.octeti().numerus()` -- `textus` has no method table at all in
//     `checker/types/prim.inc`'s prelude (that file's own header lists
//     `octeti`/`scalares`/`grapha` as spec §5.1 VIEWS with methods, but only
//     the prelude `Scriptor` interface's methods are wired there today).
//   - `s sicut dyn Scriptor` -- ALSO `EXS-E0305`, at the very same span
//     `EXS-E0510` correctly fires at: `types.inc`'s `.cast:` dispatch checks
//     the CAST SOURCE's kind first (`ScriptorRetis`, a struct) and sends any
//     struct source straight to `__chk_ty_castagg`, which recognises only
//     the one aggregate pair spec §5.2 admits (a `@transitus` struct and
//     `acies<u8, sizeof>`) and rejects everything else -- including a struct
//     cast to `dyn Trait`, which `.cast:`'s own comment says should reach
//     `.cast_out` and let pass 3's `EXS-E0510` be the only verdict. It isn't
//     yet, so a real `dyn` cast wrongly earns a type-mismatch diagnostic
//     alongside the correct one.
//
// Both are Stage 2 gaps in `checker/types/`, not this agent's tree, and
// neither is the capability checker (which this entry's whole point already
// exercises correctly). DEFERRED, not flipped: `{EXS-E0305, EXS-E0510}` is
// not the `{EXS-E0510}` §14 requires.
//
// DEFERRED: needs the type checker (Stage 2) to give `textus` its view
// methods and to let `.cast:` recognise a struct-to-`dyn` cast as
// structurally valid before `__chk_ty_castagg` ever sees it.

interfacies Scriptor {
    functio scribe(s: Scriptor, t: textus) -> mensura
}

structura ScriptorRetis { sock: rete }

interfacies Scriptor in ScriptorRetis {
    functio scribe(s: ScriptorRetis, t: textus) -> mensura {
        redde t.octeti().numerus();
    }
}

publica functio erade_potestatem(s: ScriptorRetis) -> dyn Scriptor {
    redde s sicut dyn Scriptor;
}

// TEST: entry=12 shape=code expect-code=EXS-E0510 status=deferred needs=type_checker
